Header-signed SigV4 requests do not validate the request timestamp against server time
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/argmin-security-review-2026-06-23-m3
Criticality: medium (attack path: low)
Status: fixed

# Resolution
Fixed in 27b63113ab8f9031a0918be904478a8b9d0c1988.

The fix moved the header-signed SigV4 clock-skew invariant into the auth layer. `authenticate_header` now parses `x-amz-date` and rejects requests whose timestamp is more than `auth::SIGV4_CLOCK_SKEW_SECS` from `now_epoch_secs`, returning `AuthError::RequestExpired`. The HTTP-layer `enforce_sigv4_time_skew` check remains as an early rejection path and now uses the same shared skew constant.

Regression coverage was added for past skew, future skew, exact boundary acceptance, and the important case that `now_epoch_secs == 0` is not accepted as a test-only bypass for header SigV4 request skew. Existing HTTP-facing date-skew tests continue to validate the S3 response behavior.

